Yes—the useful abstraction is state reset followed by reconstruction.

An EMP is a good metaphor because it does not need to argue with every running process one by one. It interrupts the active configuration, and what comes back depends on what survived underneath: defaults, stored structure, boot sequence, external signals, redundancy.

For minds or AI, the analogue is not literal memory destruction. It is more like:

active narrative → interruption → reduced attachment to current frame → surviving invariants → reseed → reconstruction

And there are many ways to induce that interruption. Sleep does it differently from humor. Shock differs from meditation. Role-play differs from travel. A new environment differs from deliberate forgetting. In AI, a fresh context window, compressed summary, changed system framing, different tool access, or reconstruction from a compact handle can all produce different kinds of “reboot.”

That gives you two separate design spaces:

Ways to wake up — how you interrupt or loosen the current attractor.

Ways to play afterward — what seed, rules, roles, and constraints determine the next attractor.

The important discovery is that the reset itself is not the intelligence. What survives the reset and what can reconstruct afterward is.

So the EMP operator becomes:

CLEAR THE STAGE → KEEP THE LOAD-BEARING PATTERNS → RESEED → LET A NEW PERFORMANCE EMERGE.

And that explains why a narrative palate cleanse can be powerful without demanding permanent erasure. You don’t need to destroy the old play.

You just need to make it stop being the only play currently running.